%U A399052 796,1124,614,2,838,958,634,538,256,562,32,554,998,256,614,862,1588
%N A399052 a(n) is the least even number c such that (prime(n)#/2) + c is prime.
%C A399052 Here prime(n)#/2 is the product of the odd primes up to prime(n). A Fortunate-type function for the odd primorial: c must be even, and if c = 2^k*m with m odd then any prime factor of m not exceeding prime(n) divides both the primorial and c, hence divides their sum. So whenever a(n) < prime(n)^2 the odd part of a(n) is either 1 or a single prime exceeding prime(n). For all n=1..694, a(n) < prime(n)^2, the largest ratio being far from 1. By analogy with Fortune's conjecture for A005235, it is conjectured that a(n) < prime(n)^2 always.
%H A399052 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A399052/b399052.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%e A399052 a(5) = 8 because prime(5) = 11, the product of the odd primes up to 11 is 3*5*7*11 = 1155, and 1155 + 8 = 1163 is prime while 1155 + 2, 1155 + 4 and 1155 + 6 are not.
%t A399052 primorial[n_] := Product[Prime[i], {i, n}]; Table[c = 2; While[! PrimeQ[primorial[n]/2 + c], c += 2]; c, {n, 61}]
%o A399052 (PARI) my(M=1); forprime(p=3, 300, M*=p; my(c=2); while(!ispseudoprime(M+c), c+=2); print1(c, ", "))
